    id: "7aaf12aa88cef79f932b",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Czechs rally against plan to switch funding of public media",
    summary: "Protesters say the move will stifle media freedom, while the government insists broadcasters need to cut costs.",
    preview: "Thousands of people have gathered in central Prague to protest against the Czech government's plans to scrap the licence fee and use the state budget to fund public media. Critics say the plans would allow politicians to stifle media freedom. The coalition government - which consists of the populist ANO party and two right-wing Eurosceptic parties - insists the change will not affect editorial independence, but wants broadcasters to cut costs. Parliament passed the first draft of the proposal late last month.",
    body: "Thousands of people have gathered in central Prague to protest against the Czech government's plans to scrap the licence fee and use the state budget to fund public media.\n\nCritics say the plans would allow politicians to stifle media freedom.\n\nThe coalition government - which consists of the populist ANO party and two right-wing Eurosceptic parties - insists the change will not affect editorial independence, but wants broadcasters to cut costs.\n\nParliament passed the first draft of the proposal late last month. Since then, thousands of Czechs have taken part in protests.\n\nCzech households currently pay â 205 crowns ($9.44; Â£7.06) a month for public TV and radio.\n\nSunday's protest was organised by the Million Moments for Democracy association which said \"roughly 400,000\" people had attended.\n\n\"I know that our entire Czech Television is a guarantee of truth and objectivity,\" pensioner Milana Rudolfova, 72, told Reuters news agency.\n\n\"If we do not have public television and radio then unfortunately we will not be able to believe anything anymore.\"\n\nPrime Minister Andrej Babis has insisted the government does not want to control public media.\n\n\"The funding system is changing, no one wants to control public media, no one wants to dictate to them what they should say and do, no one wants to nationalise them,\" Babis told the Czech News Agency.\n\n\"We are doing this because the system is already outdated and most of Europe is going the same way today and others are joining in,\" he added.\n\nSome European countries, including France and Norway, have moved away from the public licence fee model in the last few years.\n\nFrance removed it in 2022 and its public media, which also carries advertising, is now funded by a portion of the income from VAT.\n\nNorway abolished its license fee in 2020 and moved its public broadcaster's funding into the national budget.\n\nReuters said the proposal would amount to a 15% cut in funding for the public broadcasters Czech TV and Czech Radio.\n\nSome protesters wore pointed hats folded out of newspapers as a tribute to an animated character, Vecernicek, who has introduced Czech Television's nightly children's bedtime stories for decades.\n\nCritics have compared the government's media bill to changes in Hungary under former Prime Minister Viktor Orbán, who tightly controlled state media when he was in power.\n\nPrime Minister Andrej Babis was appointed in December in 2025, and has faced criticism over his coalition's attacks on Czech media.\n\n\"Political attacks against private and public media have become one of the hallmarks of Andrej Babis' premiership,\" said Pavol Szalai, Prague Bureau director of international press freedom organisation Reporters Without Borders, in June.\n\nRelated topics\n\n- Published9 December 2025\n\n- Published5 October 2025",
