    id: "9fef86c489562b65c4e3",
    cat: "conflitti",
    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "Daily life in Sudan paralysed by deepening fuel crisis and rising costs",
    summary: "Domestic refining has been crippled by the civil war while the Middle East conflict has led to significant fluctuations in oil prices.",
    preview: "Sudan A deepening fuel crisis is paralysing daily life across Sudan, where long lines of vehicles at the few operating petrol stations have become a daily routine. Amid the ongoing civil war, domestic refining has been crippled by drone attacks targeting infrastructure and energy and fuel sources. At the time, conflict in the Middle East since late February has led to significant fluctuations in international energy market prices.",
    body: "Sudan\n\nA deepening fuel crisis is paralysing daily life across Sudan, where long lines of vehicles at the few operating petrol stations have become a daily routine.\n\nAmid the ongoing civil war, domestic refining has been crippled by drone attacks targeting infrastructure and energy and fuel sources.\n\nAt the time, conflict in the Middle East since late February has led to significant fluctuations in international energy market prices.\n\nIn his car at a petrol garage, Osman Faisal, a Khartoum resident, said he had started queuing for petrol at seven in the morning and had been waiting for over two hours.\n\n\"I don't know how much longer I'll have to wait. I haven't been able to fill up for a week,” he said.\n\n“And the price of gasoline has further gone up recently. Now it costs about $58 per gallon. It has increased by over $8 in just several days.\"\n\nThe external shock of global oil prices has been exacerbated by the depreciation of the Sudanese pound against the US dollar, making every imported shipment more expensive.\n\nAnother Khartoum resident queuing for petrol, Adnan Osman, said he hoped the exchange rate would stabilise as soon as possible.\n\n\"Every morning when we wake up, we are forced to make a choice - go to work or queue for petrol,\" he said.\n\nIn addition to disrupting people's travel, the fuel shortage has driven up transportation and delivery costs, pushing the price of food and daily necessities higher.\n\n\"The prices of food, including vegetables and bread, are also on the rise. The life of everyone in this country is being severely affected,\" said resident Alrazy Mohamed.\n\nIt is also impacting the economy and is contributing to a worsening of the country’s humanitarian crisis.\n\nRelentless clashes since fighting erupted in April 2023 between the Sudanese Armed Forces and the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces have forced major oil fields to shut down.\n\nThe Khartoum refinery, which once met about 70 per cent of national fuel needs, has closed and several oil fields are also offline, forcing Sudan to rely almost entirely on imports.\n\nCaught between rising global prices and a plummeting local currency, petrol station operators are forced to constantly recalculate their rates and frequently shut down pumps.",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "Africanews",
    url: "http://www.africanews.com/2026/11/15/daily-life-in-sudan-paralysed-by-deepening-fuel-crisis-and-rising-costs",
    time: "10 ore fa",
